{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond III was born in 1130s. His father Raymond II Count of Tripoli died in 1152 and Raymond III succeeded him as the Count of Tripoli. His mother Hodierna of Jerusalem played a significant role in the early years of his reign.\n\n*Capture and Imprisonment*\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi during the Battle of Harim. He was imprisoned in Aleppo for ten years. During his imprisonment the regency of Tripoli was held by King Amalric I of Jerusalem and later by Raymond III's mother Hodierna of Jerusalem.\n\n*Release and Later Life*\nRaymond III was released from prison in 1174 after the death of Nur ad-Din Zengi. He returned to Tripoli and resumed his duties as the Count of Tripoli. Raymond III was a key figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in the reign of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. He was one of the leaders of the army that defeated the forces of Saladin at the Battle of Montgisard in 1177.\n\nRaymond III was also a skilled diplomat and played a key role in the negotiations between the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Byzantine Empire. He was a close ally of King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and supported his policies. Raymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli.\n\n*Personal Life*\nRaymond III married Eschiva of Bures and had no children. He was known for his wisdom and military skills. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a significant role in its politics and military campaigns.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004134069196879864}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "lo